  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the shared piece only [3mcomputes[0m validation results, or also [3mrenders[0m the errors into the DOM. That
  decides what every future form has to write for itself, and it's the part that's expensive to change later — once
  forms depend on the module touching their DOM, taking that back means editing every form.

  - [1mValidator only (pure)[0m — takes values + a rule spec, returns a result object. Each form decides how to show errors.
    Buys you a module that's trivially testable in Node with no DOM, and works for non-form validation later. Costs:
    every form repeats its own error-display code, so you may end up wanting a second helper anyway.
  - [1mValidator + DOM binding[0m — same core, plus a thin [38;5;153mbindForm(formEl, spec)[39m that wires submit, runs the rules, and
    writes messages next to the fields. Buys you genuinely less per-form code — a new form is a spec plus one call.
    Costs: the module now owns markup conventions (where the error element lives, what class it gets), which every form
    must follow, and it needs a DOM to test.
  - [1mRules declared in HTML attributes[0m — [38;5;153mdata-rules="required|email"[39m on inputs, module reads them. Buys the least JS per
    form. Costs: rules become strings parsed at runtime, awkward for cross-field rules like "confirm password matches
    password", and no way to express anything conditional.

  [1mMy recommendation: validator + DOM binding, built as two layers[0m — a pure core with the binding as a separate optional
  file on top. You get the testability of option 1 and the ergonomics of option 2, and a form that needs custom error UI
  can use the core alone. I'd skip option 3: with rules still unknown, locking them into attribute strings is the
  choice hardest to walk back.
